\section[The exam]{The exam and why its question banks mattered}
\label{sec:exam}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{RapportLOStakes}.

  The report opens with the exam as a whole --- its scoring, its banks,
  its deadline --- before any single item, because a defect can only be
  weighed against what one item counts for in this exam.  The scoring is
  met as an exercise (\cref{ex:digits}): the digit encoding is what a
  colleague is least likely to know, and reading a total oneself shows
  why partial credit is impossible faster than a stated rule would.
}%
The Python part of DD1317 is examined by a computer exam, the
\foreignlanguage{swedish}{\emph{Datorprov}}: a Canvas New Quiz of 60
minutes, written in a computer room under Safe Exam Browser.  The browser
lets the student reach the quiz and nothing else, so the tools the student
needs are embedded in the quiz: a Python terminal that runs in the browser
(Pyodide) and the Python documentation.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{The \foreignlanguage{swedish}{Datorprov}}
    The exam is a Canvas New Quiz:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item 60 minutes in a computer room, under Safe Exam Browser;
      \item a Python terminal and the Python documentation embedded in
        the quiz;
      \item 21 positions, three for each of the learning objectives 1--7;
      \item each position draws one question at random from a bank of its
        own.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

Each of the course's seven learning objectives has three positions in the
exam, and each position draws one question at random from a bank of its
own.  A student passes an objective with two of its three questions right,
and the exam with every objective passed.  Canvas gives a quiz a single
total, so the points have to carry the objectives.  Before you read how,
try it.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{exercise}\label{ex:digits}
    A question at learning objective $L$ is worth $10^{L-1}$ points, so
    full marks are 3\,333\,333.  To pass, a student needs two of the
    three questions right for every objective.
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item A student scores 3\,213\,332.  Did the student pass?
      \item Why may no question give partial credit?
    \end{enumerate}
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

The total holds one digit per objective, objective~1 in the units and
objective~7 in the millions, and the pass rule becomes \enquote{every
digit at least 2}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{solution}[Reading the digits]
    From objective~7 down to objective~1 the digits of 3\,213\,332 are 3,
    2, 1, 3, 3, 3, 2.  Objective~5 has one question right of three, so
    the student failed, high as the total looks.

    Half a point at objective~2 would be 5 points, and they land in the
    digit of objective~1.  Any fraction corrupts the digit below it, so
    every question is scored all or nothing.
  \end{solution}
\end{frame}

A grading script, \texttt{datorprov.sh}, a literate Bash program in the
course repository, reads the digits from the Canvas results.  The scheme
also fixes how much a single item weighs.  A student may get one question
of three wrong per objective, and a defective item at a position uses up
that one question for every student who draws it.  And since every student
draws a single item from each bank, a defective item hits some students
and not others.  Flawed items have been shown to change who passes: in
four medical-school exams, items that broke the standard rules for
writing them were harder, and perhaps 10--15 per cent of the students
were failed by them rather than by what they
knew.\autocite[133, 141]{Downing2005}\footnote{Across studies the
effects of flawed items are usually smaller\autocite[209]{Rodriguez2011}.
The scientific basis is in \cref{app:item-flaws}, which asks whether cues
and options that refer to other options are flaws and whether flaws
matter.}

\begin{frame}
  \begin{block}{Why the banks mattered}
    One defective item decides a digit:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item it uses up the one question a student may miss per objective;
      \item only the students who draw it are hit.
    \end{itemize}
    And the banks were hard to reach:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item 21 banks, 218 items, written by others and shared with another
        course (DD1310);
      \item Canvas's programming interface answered \texttt{Not Found}:
        the only way in was an export from the web interface, as QTI;
      \item the next sitting was two weeks away.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{block}
\end{frame}

The banks were shared with another course, DD1310, and written by others.
Canvas's programming interface, which our tools use, answered
\texttt{Not Found} for them; the only way to read them was to export the
live quiz from the web interface as a QTI archive.  The next sitting was
on 26 November 2026.  The work this report describes took place from 9 to
13 November, in one long Claude Code session, with the author steering
from the terminal and from the Canvas web interface.


\section[Find the flaw]{Find the flaw: four invented items}
\label{sec:items}

\leavevmode\ltnote{%
  Relevant learning objectives: \cref{RapportLOFlaws}.

  The colleagues judge the items before they hear about any defect
  (try first, tell later), so that the four checks of
  \cref{sec:workflow} answer something they have just tried to do.  The
  question stays the same for all four, \enquote{Would you keep the
  item?}, and does not say where to look.

  \textbf{Variation pattern}: contrast, four values of one dimension at
  once.
  \textbf{What varies} across \cref{ex:item-crash,ex:item-order,%
  ex:item-cue,ex:item-nota}: the check that exposes the flaw ---
  running the program, trying every order, measuring the bank, seeing the
  item as one student sees it.
  \textbf{What stays invariant}: a plausible item on a beginner's topic,
  shown with its key, that reads as correct.

  The items are invented because the repository is public; each copies
  the flaw of a real item, not its content.
}%
Before the workflow, four items for you to judge.  They are invented ---
no real exam question appears in this report --- but each has a flaw we
found in the real banks.  Answer each item, then decide whether you would
keep it on the exam.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[Item 1]\label{ex:item-crash}
    \mode<presentation>{\setminted{fontsize=\scriptsize}}%
    The item shows a program:
<<[[vowels.py]]>>=
def count_vowels(word):
    """Return the number of vowels in word"""
    count = 0
    for letter in word:
        if letter in vowels:
            count += 1
    return count

def main():
    """Print the number of vowels in a word"""
    vowels = "aeiouy"
    print(count_vowels("banana"))

main()
@
    It asks: \enquote{What does it print?}  Options: (A)~\texttt{3},
    (B)~\texttt{0}, (C)~\texttt{6}, (D)~an error message; key: A.  Would
    you keep the item?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

The item looks like an ordinary reading exercise: a loop, a condition, a
counter.  The quickest way to answer it is also the way to check it.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{solution}[Item 1: the key crashes]
    Running the program settles it:
    \runpython[showcommand]{examples/vowels.py}
    The key is wrong: the right answer is D.
  \end{solution}
\end{frame}

The variable [[vowels]] is local to [[main]], and [[count_vowels]] cannot
see it.  The item's author read the program as if it could --- the very
misconception about scope that an item at this objective might test.  In
the real banks one keyed program used the caller's variables and stopped
with a [[NameError]]; another never printed its \enquote{not found}
message, because a flag was set outside the [[if]] that should have set
it.  A reader who reads such a program the way its author meant it sees
nothing wrong; anyone who runs it sees the defect at once.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{exercise}[Item 2]\label{ex:item-order}
    The item asks you to put these lines in order, so that the program
    prints \texttt{27}:
\begin{minted}{python}
print(total)
for number in numbers:
total = 0
    total += number
numbers = [4, 8, 15]
\end{minted}
    The key is the order in which the lines must be.  Would you keep the
    item?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

An ordering item, also called a Parsons problem, is graded against the one
order its author keyed.  That is safe only if no other order works.

\begin{frame}[fragile]
  \begin{solution}[Item 2: two orders work]
    A program that tries every order of the lines finds two that print
    \texttt{27}:
    \runpython[showcommand]{examples/parsons.py}
  \end{solution}
\end{frame}

Canvas accepts one of them; a student who chooses the other gets no
points for a working program.

Ordering items, also called Parsons problems, are used on programming
exams, and in the first study of them their scores correlated notably
with the scores for writing code,\autocite{denny2008} though later work
shows that the two are not interchangeable.\autocite{wu2024,newar2025}
That an ordering item can have more than one valid order is known: its
first describers called such ambiguous solutions perhaps the biggest
problem of assessing Parsons problems automatically, when the assessment
is based on the order of the lines.\autocite[IIP-127]{ihantola2011}%
\footnote{The scientific basis is in \cref{app:parsons}, which asks how
ordering items are used in exams and whether one order is enough.}
Two assignments whose order does not matter are one way for an ordering
item to get a second solution.  The real banks also had the
opposite: items where no order worked, because one line carried a stray
apostrophe or a double space that the key did not.  The program that found
the two orders is developed in \cref{sec:checker}.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{exercise}[Item 3]\label{ex:item-cue}
    The item asks: \enquote{What happens when a function reaches its end
    without a \texttt{return} statement?}  The options are
    \begin{itemize}
      \item[A] It returns \texttt{0}.
      \item[B] It raises an error.
      \item[C] It returns the last value it computed.
      \item[D] It returns \texttt{None}, which the caller gets as the
        value of the call.
    \end{itemize}
    and the key is D.  Would you keep the item?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

Item~3 is correct: its key is right, and every distractor is wrong.  Its
flaw is in the form of the options.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{solution}[Item 3: the key is the longest option]
    A student who knows nothing about return values can still pick D: it
    is the longest option and the only qualified one.  In one item that
    is a small cue.  In a bank it is a pattern:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item in two real banks the key was the longest option in nine of 11
        and in eight of 10 items;
      \item in a third, the same pattern of correct answers held in six of
        its seven items.
    \end{itemize}
  \end{solution}
\end{frame}

Item-writing guidelines advise keeping the options about equal in
length and free of clues to the key.\autocite{HaladynaDowningRodriguez2002}
The advice rests mostly on consensus rather than
tests,\autocite[204]{Rodriguez2011} but where it has been tested, a
longer key made items easier,\autocite[209]{Rodriguez2011} and in a
series of experiments examinees answered cued items right far more often
when they could compare the options than when they saw them one at a
time.\autocite[247]{Willing2015}\footnote{Other studies of the length
cue found weak or no effects.\autocite{Martinez2009,Casu2019} The
scientific basis, and its reservations, are in \cref{app:item-flaws}.}
A cue like this one is found by looking at the bank as a test-wise student
would, not at a single item: count how often the key is the longest
option, the only one written in the course's style, or the odd one out in
a near-identical pair.  The fix is a matter of design: make the
distractors as specific as the key, or the key as plain as the
distractors.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{exercise}[Item 4]\label{ex:item-nota}
    The item asks: \enquote{Which of these can you use as a variable name
    in Python?}  Its author wrote the options in this order:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item[A] \texttt{2nd\_place}
      \item[B] \texttt{my-score}
      \item[C] \texttt{class}
      \item[D] None of the above
    \end{itemize}
    The key is D.  Canvas shuffles the options for each student.  Would
    you keep the item?
  \end{exercise}
\end{frame}

The item is correct as its author wrote it: [[2nd_place]] starts with a
digit, [[my-score]] holds a minus sign and [[class]] is a keyword.  But
the author's order is not the order the students see.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{solution}[Item 4: \enquote{above} after shuffling]
    One student sees the options in this order:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item[A] None of the above
      \item[B] \texttt{class}
      \item[C] \texttt{2nd\_place}
      \item[D] \texttt{my-score}
    \end{itemize}
    For this student \enquote{the above} is nothing at all.  An option
    that refers to the other options depends on their order, and the
    platform does not keep the order.
  \end{solution}
\end{frame}

The guidelines advise avoiding \enquote{all of the above} and using
\enquote{none of the above} with care,\autocite[205--206]{Rodriguez2011}
and the studies disagree about the harm: in one experiment \enquote{none
of the above} made items harder only when it was the
key.\autocite{Pachai2015}\footnote{A formal analysis finds no harm in it
at all.\autocite[271]{Persson2025} The scientific basis is in
\cref{app:item-flaws}.}  Shuffling gives a reason that holds whatever one
thinks of those.  The rewritten banks have
no option that refers to other options.

\begin{frame}
  \begin{summary}
    Four flaws, and four checks that expose them:
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item run the program;
      \item try every order;
      \item measure the bank;
      \item look at the item as one student sees it.
    \end{enumerate}
    Reading an item the way its author meant it shows none of them.
  \end{summary}
\end{frame}


